==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|moment|ous}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/məʊˈmɛn.təs/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/moʊˈmɛn.təs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-momentous.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛntəs|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[outstanding|Outstanding]] in [[importance]], of great [[consequence]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1725|author=[[w:Daniel Defoe|Daniel Defoe]]|title=Everybody's Business is Nobody's Business
|passage=The reason why I did not publish this book till the end of the last sessions of parliament was, because I did not care to interfere with more '''momentous''' affairs.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1831|author=[[w:James Fenimore Cooper|James Fenimore Cooper]]|title=Homeward Bound|chapter=31
|passage="It has been a '''momentous''' month, and I hope we shall all retain healthful recollections of it as long as we live."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=[[w:Joseph Conrad|Joseph Conrad]]|title=The End of the Tether|chapter=3
|passage=What to the other parties was merely the sale of a ship was to him a '''momentous''' event involving a radically new view of existence.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=July 1 2007|author=[[w:Richard Dawkins|Richard Dawkins]]|title=Inferior Design|titleurl=http://www.nytimes.com/2007/07/01/books/review/Dawkins-t.html?pagewanted=all|journal=New York Times|accessdate=19 Nov 2013
|passage=Natural selection is arguably the most '''momentous''' idea ever to occur to a human mind, because it — alone as far as we know — explains the elegant illusion of design that pervades the living kingdoms and explains, in passing, us.}}

